(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_zoology_locomotionandmovement=[{id:"neet-2026r-q091",pn:91,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Human vertebral column",q:"The number of vertebrae in a human is ________.",o:["12","26","206","7"],c:1,s:`The number of vertebrae in an adult human is 26.
Note: Printed under the BIOLOGY heading at 91 (booklet order says Botany); question content is Zoology, so sub set to Zoology.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q107",pn:107,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Bones of lower limb",q:"Which of the following represents the correct sequence of arrangement of bones in the lower limb of humans?",o:["Patella-femur-tibia-tarsal","Femur-patella-tibia-tarsal","Femur-tarsal-patella-tibia","Femur-tibia-patella-tarsal"],c:1,s:`Each hindlimb has 30 bones.
The lower limb bones are femur (thigh bone), patella, tibia and fibula, tarsals (ankle bones), metatarsals and phalanges.
The cup-shaped patella (knee cap) covers the knee ventrally.
So the sequence is femur - patella - tibia - tarsal.
Note: Question sits in the Botany block of the booklet but is Zoology content; sub set to Zoology.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q152",pn:152,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Vertebral column",q:"Which of the following is the correct order of arrangement of vertebrate column from the head to toe?",o:["Sacrum, lumbar vertebra, thoracic vertebra, cervical vertebra","Cervical vertebra, lumbar vertebra, thoracic vertebra, sacrum","Cervical vertebra, thoracic vertebra, lumbar vertebra, sacrum","Cervical vertebra, thoracic vertebra, sacrum, lumbar vertebra"],c:2,s:`The vertebral column is formed by 26 serially arranged units called vertebrae and lies on the dorsal side.
Starting from the skull it has cervical (7), thoracic (12), lumbar (5), sacral (1, fused) and coccygeal (1, fused) regions.
So the order is cervical, thoracic, lumbar, sacrum and coccyx.`},{id:"neet-2026-q158",pn:158,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Muscular and skeletal disorders",q:`Match List I with List II related to muscular/skeletal system:
List I \u2014 List II
A. Tetany \u2014 I. Inflammation of joints
B. Arthritis \u2014 II. Autoimmune disorder affecting neuromuscular junction
C. Myasthenia gravis \u2014 III. Wild contraction in muscle due to low Ca\u207A\u207A in body fluid
D. Muscular dystrophy \u2014 IV. Progressive degeneration of skeletal muscle`,o:["A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV"],c:0,s:`Arthritis is an inflammation of joints.
Tetany is rapid spasm (wild contraction) in muscle due to low Ca\u207A\u207A in body fluids.
Muscular dystrophy is progressive degeneration of skeletal muscle, mostly due to a genetic disorder.
Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disorder affecting the neuromuscular junction. It leads to fatigue, weakening and paralysis of skeletal muscle.
Hence A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV is correct.`},{id:"neet-2026-q168",pn:168,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Human skeleton",q:`Which of the following statements are correct with reference to human endoskeleton?
A. Human skull is monocondylic.
B. The joint between any two adjoining vertebrae is a cartilaginous joint.
C. In human beings, the number of cervical vertebrae is seven.
D. All ribs except the last 2 pairs are bicephalic.
E. The occipital bone of skull is articulated with atlas vertebra.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A, B and D only","B and E only","B, C and E only","C, D and E only"],c:2,s:`A is incorrect: the human skull is dicondylic. It articulates with the atlas (top of the vertebral column) through two occipital condyles.
B is correct: the joint between adjacent vertebrae is a cartilaginous joint.
C is correct: humans have 7 cervical vertebrae.
D is incorrect: all ribs are bicephalic, i.e. each has two articulation surfaces on its dorsal end.
E is correct: the occipital bone of the skull articulates with the atlas vertebra through the occipital condyles, forming the atlanto-occipital joint.
So B, C and E are correct.`},{id:"neet-2026-q173",pn:173,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Muscle contraction",q:`Choose the correct statements regarding muscle contraction.
A. A motor neuron carries a signal sent by the Central Nervous System (CNS) to the sarcolemma of the muscle fibre.
B. The neural signal generates an action potential which causes the release of Ca\u207A\u207A into sarcoplasm.
C. Increase in Ca\u207A\u207A inactivates the actin for breaking cross bridges.
D. Actin binds to the myosin head to form a cross bridge.
E. Shortening of sarcomere takes place, by pulling actin filaments towards the centre of 'A' band.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A, B, D and E only","C and D only","C and E only","A and B only"],c:0,s:`Statements A, B, D and E are correct. Statement C is incorrect.
A neural signal reaching the neuromuscular junction releases a neurotransmitter (acetylcholine).
This generates an action potential in the sarcolemma, which spreads through the muscle fibre and releases Ca\u207A\u207A into the sarcoplasm.
Ca\u207A\u207A binds to the subunit of troponin on actin filaments and removes the masking of the active site for myosin on actin.
This lets the cross bridge form.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q179",pn:179,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Muscle contraction",q:"Muscle contraction is initiated by a signal sent by the central nervous system by the release of _____ .",o:["acetyl coenzyme A","cyclic guanine monophosphate","cyclic adenine monophosphate","acetyl choline"],c:3,s:`The CNS sends the signal through a motor neuron.
The junction of a motor neuron and the sarcolemma of the muscle fibre is the neuromuscular junction (motor-end plate).
A signal reaching it releases a neurotransmitter, acetylcholine.
Acetylcholine generates an action potential in the sarcolemma.`},{id:"neet-2024-q158",pn:158,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Types of muscle",q:`Three types of muscles are given as a, b and c. Identify the correct matching pair along with their location in human body :
Name of muscle/location`,o:["(a) Skeletal - Biceps; (b) Involuntary - Intestine; (c) Smooth - Heart.","(a) Involuntary - Nose tip; (b) Skeletal - Bone; (c) Cardiac - Heart.","(a) Smooth - Toes; (b) Skeletal - Legs; (c) Cardiac - Heart.","(a) Skeletal - Triceps; (b) Smooth - Stomach; (c) Cardiac - Heart."],c:3,s:`Figure (a) is skeletal muscle, attached to skeletal bones; in biceps and triceps striated fibres lie bundled in parallel.
Figure (b) is smooth muscle, found in walls of internal organs like blood vessels, stomach and intestine.
Figure (c) is cardiac muscle, found only in the heart.
So option (4).`,fig:{w:260,h:180,alt:"Three muscle fibre drawings: (a) thick parallel cross-striated fibres with nuclei, (b) thin spindle-shaped unstriated fibres, (c) branched striated fibres with dark cross bands.",d:[["r",5,10,24,140],["l",5,18,29,18],["l",5,25,29,25],["l",5,32,29,32],["l",5,39,29,39],["l",5,46,29,46],["l",5,53,29,53],["l",5,60,29,60],["l",5,67,29,67],["l",5,74,29,74],["l",5,81,29,81],["l",5,88,29,88],["l",5,95,29,95],["l",5,102,29,102],["l",5,109,29,109],["l",5,116,29,116],["l",5,123,29,123],["l",5,130,29,130],["l",5,137,29,137],["l",5,144,29,144],["r",33,10,24,140],["l",33,18,57,18],["l",33,25,57,25],["l",33,32,57,32],["l",33,39,57,39],["l",33,46,57,46],["l",33,53,57,53],["l",33,60,57,60],["l",33,67,57,67],["l",33,74,57,74],["l",33,81,57,81],["l",33,88,57,88],["l",33,95,57,95],["l",33,102,57,102],["l",33,109,57,109],["l",33,116,57,116],["l",33,123,57,123],["l",33,130,57,130],["l",33,137,57,137],["l",33,144,57,144],["r",60,10,24,140],["l",60,18,84,18],["l",60,25,84,25],["l",60,32,84,32],["l",60,39,84,39],["l",60,46,84,46],["l",60,53,84,53],["l",60,60,84,60],["l",60,67,84,67],["l",60,74,84,74],["l",60,81,84,81],["l",60,88,84,88],["l",60,95,84,95],["l",60,102,84,102],["l",60,109,84,109],["l",60,116,84,116],["l",60,123,84,123],["l",60,130,84,130],["l",60,137,84,137],["l",60,144,84,144],["c",17,40,2.5],["c",45,80,2.5],["c",72,55,2.5],["c",17,110,2.5],["p","M100 10 C94 60 94 100 100 150 C106 100 106 60 100 10Z"],["p","M118 14 C111 60 111 100 118 146 C125 100 125 60 118 14Z"],["p","M136 8 C130 60 130 100 136 152 C142 100 142 60 136 8Z"],["p","M152 16 C147 59 147 99 152 142 C157 99 157 59 152 16Z"],["p","M108 40 C103 75 103 115 108 150 C113 115 113 75 108 40Z"],["p","M144 30 C139 68 139 108 144 146 C149 108 149 68 144 30Z"],["c",118,70,1.6],["c",136,90,1.6],["c",104,100,1.6],["r",180,8,22,142],["r",224,30,22,120],["r",202,52,22,56],["l",180,16,202,16,"B"],["l",180,25,202,25,"B"],["l",180,34,202,34,"B"],["l",224,34,246,34,"B"],["l",180,43,202,43,"B"],["l",224,43,246,43,"B"],["l",180,52,202,52,"B"],["l",224,52,246,52,"B"],["l",180,61,202,61,"B"],["l",224,61,246,61,"B"],["l",180,70,202,70,"B"],["l",224,70,246,70,"B"],["l",180,79,202,79,"B"],["l",224,79,246,79,"B"],["l",180,88,202,88,"B"],["l",224,88,246,88,"B"],["l",180,97,202,97,"B"],["l",224,97,246,97,"B"],["l",180,106,202,106,"B"],["l",224,106,246,106,"B"],["l",180,115,202,115,"B"],["l",224,115,246,115,"B"],["l",180,124,202,124,"B"],["l",224,124,246,124,"B"],["l",180,133,202,133,"B"],["l",224,133,246,133,"B"],["l",180,142,202,142,"B"],["l",224,142,246,142,"B"],["l",202,64,224,64,"B"],["l",202,80,224,80,"B"],["l",202,96,224,96,"B"],["t",30,172,"(a)","m"],["t",126,172,"(b)","m"],["t",214,172,"(c)","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2024-q174",pn:174,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Types of joints",q:`Match List I with List II:
A. Fibrous joints \u2014 I. Adjacent vertebrae, limited movement
B. Cartilaginous joints \u2014 II. Humerus and Pectoral girdle, rotational movement
C. Hinge joints \u2014 III. Skull, don't allow any movement
D. Ball and socket joints \u2014 IV. Knee, help in locomotion`,o:["A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV"],c:1,s:`Fibrous joints allow no movement; flat skull bones join end to end by dense fibrous tissue as sutures.
Cartilaginous joints lie between adjacent vertebrae and allow limited movement.
Hinge joints are synovial joints in the knee that help locomotion.
Ball and socket joints are synovial joints between humerus and pectoral girdle and allow rotation.
So A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II.
Note: The printed answer is [2], but the printed solution names option (4); the working agrees with option (2).`},{id:"neet-2023-q155",pn:155,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Types of joints",q:`Match List I with List II.
List I (Type of Joint): A. Cartilaginous Joint; B. Ball and Socket joint; C. Fibrous Joint; D. Saddle Joint
List II (Found between): I. Between flat skull bones; II. Between adjacent vertebrae in vertebral column; III. Between carpal and metacarpal of thumb; IV. Between Humerus and Pectoral girdle
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],c:2,s:`Cartilaginous joints lie between adjacent vertebrae (A-II).
Ball and socket joint is between humerus and pectoral girdle (B-IV).
Fibrous joints join the flat skull bones (C-I).
Saddle joint is between carpal and metacarpal of the thumb (D-III).`},{id:"neet-2023-q197",pn:197,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Skeletal muscle structure",q:`Which of the following statements are correct regarding skeletal muscle?
A. Muscle bundles are held together by collagenous connective tissue layer called fascicle.
B. Sarcoplasmic reticulum of muscle fibre is a store house of calcium ions.
C. Striated appearance of skeletal muscle fibre is due to distribution pattern of actin and myosin proteins.
D. M line is considered as functional unit of contraction called sarcomere.
Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["C and D only","A, B and C only","B and C only","A, C and D only"],c:2,s:`B and C are correct.
A is wrong: the layer holding bundles together is fascia; the bundles are fascicles.
D is wrong: the sarcomere (functional unit) is the part of the myofibril between two successive Z lines.`},{id:"neet-2022-q159",pn:159,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Joints of vertebral column",q:"Which of the following is present between the adjacent bones of the vertebral column?",o:["Cartilage","Areolar tissue","Smooth muscle","Intercalated discs"],c:0,s:`Cartilage forms the intervertebral disc between adjacent vertebrae. This is a cartilaginous joint.
Areolar tissue is loose connective tissue under the skin.
Smooth muscle is found in visceral organs.
Intercalated discs are a feature of cardiac muscle.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q163",pn:163,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Sliding filament theory",q:"According to the sliding filament theory",o:["The actin filaments slide away from A-band resulting in shortening of sarcomere.","Actin and myosin filaments slide over each other to increase the length of the sarcomere.","Length of A-band does not change.","I-band increases in length."],c:2,s:`In contraction the thin filaments (actin) slide over the thick filaments (myosin).
The I band becomes shorter and the A band keeps its length.
The Z discs come closer, so the sarcomere shortens.`},{id:"neet-2022-q165",pn:165,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Osteoporosis",q:`Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A): Osteoporosis is characterised by decreased bone mass and increased chance of fractures.
Reason (R): Common cause of osteoporosis is increased levels of estrogen.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is correct but (R) is not correct","(A) is not correct but (R) is correct","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)"],c:1,s:`Osteoporosis is an age-related disorder with lower bone mass, so fractures become more likely. A is correct.
Its cause is decreased oestrogen, not increased. R is incorrect.`},{id:"neet-2022-q179",pn:179,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Muscular and skeletal disorders",q:"Which of the following is a correct match for disease and its symptoms?",o:["Tetany \u2013 High Ca2+ level causing rapid spasms.","Myasthenia gravis \u2013 Genetic disorder resulting in weakening and paralysis of skeletal muscle","Muscular dystrophy \u2013 An auto immune disorder causing progressive degeneration of skeletal muscle","Arthritis \u2013 Inflammed joints"],c:3,s:`Arthritis is inflammation of joints, so (4) is correct.
Tetany is rapid muscle spasm due to LOW Ca2+ in body fluid.
Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disorder of the neuromuscular junction causing fatigue, weakness and paralysis of skeletal muscle.
Muscular dystrophy is progressive degeneration of skeletal muscle, mostly genetic.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q182",pn:182,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Disorders of joints",q:"Gout is a type of disorder which leads to :",o:["Weakening of bones due to low calcium level","Inflammation of joints due to accumulation of uric acid crystals","Weakening of bones due to decreased bone mass","Inflammation of joints due to cartilage degeneration"],c:1,s:`Gout is a metabolic disease with extra uric acid production.
Uric acid crystals collect in the joints and cause inflammation.`},{id:"neet-2021-q159",pn:159,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Muscle types",q:"Which of the following statements wrongly represents the nature of smooth muscle?",o:["These muscles are present in the wall of blood vessels","These muscle have no striations","They are involuntary muscles","Communication among the cells is performed by intercalated discs"],c:3,s:`Smooth muscle is non-striated, involuntary and lines vessel walls, gut and uterus.
Intercalated discs occur only in cardiac muscle, so statement 4 is wrong.`},{id:"neet-2021-q160",pn:160,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Muscular disorders",q:"Chronic auto immune disorder affecting neuro muscular junction leading to fatigue, weakening and paralysis of skeletal muscle is called as :",o:["Gout","Arthritis","Muscular dystrophy","Myasthenia gravis"],c:3,s:`Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disease at the neuromuscular junction that causes fatigue and weakness of skeletal muscle.
Gout is uric acid crystals in joints, arthritis is joint inflammation and muscular dystrophy is a genetic degeneration of muscle.`},{id:"neet-2021-q190",pn:190,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Muscle contraction",q:`During muscular contraction which of the following events occur?
(a) 'H' zone disappears (b) 'A' band widens (c) 'I' band reduces in width (d) Myosine hydrolyzes ATP, releasing the ADP and Pi. (e) Z-lines attached to actins are pulled inwards.`,o:["(b), (d), (e), (a) only","(a), (c), (d), (e) only","(a), (b), (c), (d) only","(b), (c), (d), (e) only"],c:1,s:`Myosin heads hydrolyse ATP and release ADP and Pi as they pull actin: (d) occurs.
Actin filaments slide inward, pulling the Z-lines closer: (e) occurs.
The H zone narrows and disappears, and the I band gets shorter: (a) and (c) occur.
The A band keeps the same length, so (b) is wrong.
So (a), (c), (d), (e).`},{id:"neet-2021-q191",pn:191,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Bones and joints",q:`Match List-I with List-II
List-I: (a) Scapula (b) Cranium (c) Sternum (d) Vertebral
List-II: (i) Cartilaginous joints (ii) Flat bone (iii) Fibrous joints (iv) Triangular flat bone`,o:["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],c:0,s:`The scapula is a triangular flat bone: a-iv.
Skull bones of the cranium are joined by fibrous joints (sutures): b-iii.
The sternum is a flat bone: c-ii.
Vertebrae are joined by cartilaginous joints: d-i.`}]})();
